# Root Cause Analysis (RCA)

## Issue Summary

`eksctl create iamserviceaccount` failed on an EC2 instance attempting to communicate with an EKS cluster (`three-tier`) in `us-west-2`.

---

## Timeline of Errors & Fixes

| # | Error | Root Cause | Fix Applied |
|---|-------|-----------|-------------|
| 1 | `dial tcp 172.31.36.118:443: i/o timeout` | Cluster security group not allowing inbound port 443 from EC2 | Added inbound rule to cluster SG allowing EC2's SG on port 443 |
| 2 | `connection to localhost:8080 was refused` | No kubeconfig configured on the EC2 instance | Ran `aws eks update-kubeconfig` |
| 3 | `Unauthorized` → `is forbidden: User cannot get resource` | IAM user `oregonuser` had no EKS access entry granting cluster permissions | Created EKS access entry with `AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy` |

---

## Root Cause Details

### 1. Network — Security Group Blocking

- The EKS cluster security group (`sg-06ff66425bc1d820d`) had no inbound rule for port 443 from the EC2 instance's security group (`sg-0461d60b8da98692b`)
- Even though both were in the same VPC and subnet, the cluster SG must explicitly allow traffic

### 2. Configuration — Missing kubeconfig

- `kubectl` defaults to `localhost:8080` when no kubeconfig exists
- The EC2 instance never had the kubeconfig generated for the EKS cluster

### 3. Authorization — No EKS Access Entry

- The IAM user (`oregonuser`) was not the identity that created the cluster (likely created via console with a different role)
- EKS does not grant access to IAM identities by default — they must be explicitly added via access entries or `aws-auth` ConfigMap

---

## Fixes Applied

```bash
# Fix 1: Security group inbound rule
aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --region us-west-2 \
  --group-id sg-06ff66425bc1d820d \
  --protocol tcp --port 443 \
  --source-group sg-0461d60b8da98692b

# Fix 2: Generate kubeconfig
aws eks update-kubeconfig --name three-tier --region us-west-2

# Fix 3: Grant cluster admin access
aws eks create-access-entry --region us-west-2 \
  --cluster-name three-tier \
  --principal-arn arn:aws:iam::307946644949:user/oregonuser \
  --type STANDARD

aws eks associate-access-policy --region us-west-2 \
  --cluster-name three-tier \
  --principal-arn arn:aws:iam::307946644949:user/oregonuser \
  --policy-arn arn:aws:eks::aws:cluster-access-policy/AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy \
  --access-scope type=cluster
```

---

## Future Prevention Checklist

When setting up a new EKS cluster and EC2 management instance, verify these before running any `kubectl`/`eksctl` commands:

### 1. Network Connectivity Check
```bash
# Verify the cluster SG allows your EC2 on port 443
aws ec2 describe-security-group-rules --region us-west-2 \
  --filters "Name=group-id,Values=<cluster-sg-id>" \
  --query "SecurityGroupRules[?ToPort==\`443\`]"
```

### 2. Kubeconfig Check
```bash
# Ensure kubeconfig exists and points to the right cluster
cat ~/.kube/config
# Or regenerate:
aws eks update-kubeconfig --name <cluster-name> --region <region>
```

### 3. IAM Authorization Check
```bash
# Verify your identity
aws sts get-caller-identity

# Check if your identity has an access entry
aws eks list-access-entries --region us-west-2 --cluster-name <cluster-name>

# Quick connectivity + auth test
kubectl get svc
```

### 4. One-liner Connectivity Diagnostic
```bash
# If kubectl fails, this tells you which layer is broken:
curl -sk --connect-timeout 5 $(kubectl config view --minify -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.server}') && echo "Network OK" || echo "Network BLOCKED"
```

