roblem
Three pods (cart, catalogue, user) stuck in CrashLoopBackOff with 6+ restarts while other pods were running fine.

Troubleshooting Steps
Step 1: Check Pod Logs

kubectl logs cart-78b5488766-t9rld -n robot-shop --tail=20
Error:


ReferenceError: performance is not defined
    at /otel-auto-instrumentation-nodejs/node_modules/@opentelemetry/core/...
Conclusion: Crash originates from injected OpenTelemetry libraries, not the application.

Step 2: Check What's Injecting the Libraries

kubectl get instrumentation -n robot-shop
→ No Instrumentation CRD found


kubectl get deployment cart -n robot-shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.metadata.annotations}' | python3 -m json.tool
→ Found annotations triggering auto-instrumentation:


{
    "instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-nodejs": "true",
    "instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-java": "true",
    "instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-python": "true",
    "instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-dotnet": "true",
    "cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-*": "true"
}
Root Cause
The Helm chart includes annotations that trigger OpenTelemetry webhook-based auto-instrumentation. Init containers inject OpenTelemetry SDK libraries that require a newer Node.js version than what the cart, catalogue, and user containers use. The injected SDK calls performance (a global available in Node.js 16+), causing an immediate crash on older runtimes.

Fix Applied
Disabled the annotations by setting them to "false":


kubectl patch deployment cart -n robot-shop -p '{"spec":{"template":{"metadata":{"annotations":{"instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-nodejs":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-java":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-python":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-dotnet":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-nodejs":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-java":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-python":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-dotnet":"false"}}}}}'

kubectl patch deployment catalogue -n robot-shop -p '{"spec":{"template":{"metadata":{"annotations":{"instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-nodejs":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-java":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-python":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-dotnet":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-nodejs":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-java":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-python":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-dotnet":"false"}}}}}'

kubectl patch deployment user -n robot-shop -p '{"spec":{"template":{"metadata":{"annotations":{"instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-nodejs":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-java":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-python":"false","instrumentation.opentelemetry.io/inject-dotnet":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-nodejs":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-java":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-python":"false","cloudwatch.aws.amazon.com/auto-annotate-dotnet":"false"}}}}}'
No manual pod restart needed — patching the deployment template triggers an automatic rolling update.

Diagnostic Flow

Pods in CrashLoopBackOff
    ↓
kubectl logs → Error from /otel-auto-instrumentation-nodejs/
    ↓
Injected library crash, not application code
    ↓
Check init containers → 4 OpenTelemetry init containers present
    ↓
Check deployment annotations → inject-* set to "true"
    ↓
Set annotations to "false" → Pods restart without injection → All Running ✅

